  "scenario": "A global logistics carrier moves packages through pickup, transit, customs, and last-mile delivery across thousands of hubs and carrier partners, generating more than 6 million shipment scan events daily — pickups, hub scans, customs clearances, delivery attempts, and exceptions.\n\nBecause the existing tracking system synchronized in batches, shipment status was chronically stale. During a recent peak-season surge, a spike of events overwhelmed the pipeline; events were lost and duplicated, causing incorrect delivery statuses and missed SLA-breach alerts, and customers received no real-time notice when a package was falling behind or a delivery attempt had failed.\n\nThe mandate is a real-time, event-driven shipment tracking platform on a serverless Amazon API Gateway → AWS Lambda → Amazon DynamoDB architecture, with streaming ingestion, queue-based resilience, SLA-breach and delivery-failure notifications, an analytics sink, and microservice-level tracing.\n\nThe outgoing engineering team left behind a Java Spring Boot application packaged as an AWS Lambda container image with empty handler business logic (an API handler and a stream processor), incomplete DynamoDB repository methods, and empty SLA-breach/delivery-failure notification logic. A completed Dockerfile and sample payloads are available, and supporting AWS resources are pre-created.\n\nYour responsibility is to complete the application logic and integrate all AWS services below.",
